From d37db66e63a39c089e5bb36ea7eae995ac13454b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lmoresi <louis.moresi@anu.edu.au>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 04:21:25 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] add_fault refuses a cut with no facet, or one that misses
 part of the trace

The cut labels an edge only when both its ends and its midpoint lie on the
trace. A trace sampled finer than the mesh can leave a sample without a
vertex, and the chord between its neighbours is then unlabelled. add_fault
returned such faults, with no facet or with a chain stopping short of the
trace's end, and said nothing (UNFILED 68). On an irregular 1/12 box, 8 of
40 smooth traces sampled 0.3 cells apart came back with no facet, and 4
stopped short.

After each segment's cut, _refuse_an_empty_or_partial_cut raises when the
cut labelled no facet, or when some point of the trace (ten per segment) is
more than half a cell from the labelled chain. The cell is the mean edge
length within the trace's bounding box, grown by a tenth of its diagonal.
The message gives the trace's median sample spacing against that cell size,
and the fix: resample to about one cell apart. Counts and distances are
reduced over the ranks, so every rank reaches the same verdict.

The label-only path (cut=False) is unchanged: there an empty label is
legitimate (a fault wholly inside the seam ligament).

test_0897:
- an empty and a short chain from traces sampled 0.3 cells apart on the
  regular box, which fail on development;
- a trace sampled a cell apart, which is still added;
- the check itself on chains labelled by hand along y = 0.5, which does not
  depend on how any mesher places a cut.
The fault suite (38 files) is unchanged: 417 passed, 1 skipped.

Underworld development team with AI support from Claude Code
